      "text": "According to the “use it or lose it” hypothesis of cognitive aging, cognitive enrichment and cognitively engaging activities are associated with the maintenance of high levels of cognitive functioning in old age (for an overview, see Hertzog, Kramer, Wilson, & Lindenberger, 2008; for a debate, see Salthouse, 2006; Schooler, 2007). Similar ideas have been brought forward with respect to characteristics of individuals’ work environment (Schooler, 1984; Willis & Schaie, 2006), with more cognitively enriching work demands providing an optimal environment for cognitive development and maintenance. Theoretically, engaging with complex environmental demands with greater decision making and control by the worker are considered beneficial to cognitive functioning, while simple environments with routinized work are considered detrimental to cognitive functioning (Kohn & Schooler, 1978). Among",
